#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# echo_timing

#% text
# `echo_timing(e)` prints the wall-clock seconds the evaluation of `e` took and gives the value of `e`; `echo_timing(e, label)` prints them after the label.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The argument is held and timed here, so the time is that of its own evaluation. `timing(e)` gives the seconds and the value as a list instead of printing.

#% item
# The time depends on the machine and its load, so this page shows no printed value: `length(echo_timing(range(10)))` prints a number of seconds and gives 10.

#% item
# The value comes back, so `echo_timing` can be put around any part of a computation.


#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The first argument is held, so it is evaluated inside the timing:

#% cell
attributes(echo_timing)
#% output
#= [hold_first]
#% section
# See also

#% text
# `timing`, `echo`, `echo_evaluation`
